WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.630
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:09.620
In computer graphics, we use the term shading
to describe the process of calculating the

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:15.759
appearance of a material. This gives the heart
and soul of most graphical systems that visualize

00:00:15.759 --> 00:00:21.150
something on our screen. Let the blue sphere
be the object to be shaded and the red patch

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.600
be the light source illuminating it.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.630
The question is, in this configuration, how
should the blue sphere look in reality?

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:33.510
In order to obtain high-quality images, we
need to calculate how much of the red patch

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:38.370
is visible from the blue sphere. This describes
the object's relation to the light source.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:43.999
It it close or is it nearby? Is it facing
the object or not? What shape is the light

00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:49.489
source? These factors determine how much light
will arrive to the surface of the blue sphere.

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:53.600
This is what mathematicians like to call an
integration problem.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.679
However, beyond this calculation we also have
to take into consideration the reflectance

00:00:58.679 --> 00:01:03.309
of the material that the blue sphere is made
of. Whether we have a white wall surface or

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:09.189
an orange makes a great deal of difference
and throws a wrench in our already complex calculations.

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:14.350
The final shading is the product of this visibility
situation and the material properties of the

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:20.479
sphere. Needless to say that the mathematical
description of many materials can get extremely

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:24.130
complex, which makes our calculations really
time consuming.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.630
In this piece of work, a technique is proposed
that can approximate these two factors in

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:29.619
real time.

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:34.640
The paper contains a very detailed demonstration
of the difference between this and the analytical

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.840
computations that give us the perfect results
but take extremely long. In short, this technique

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:46.789
is very closely matching the analytic results,
but it is doing it in real time. I really

00:01:46.789 --> 00:01:51.579
don't know what to say. We're used to wait
for hours to obtain images like this, and

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:58.429
now, 15 milliseconds per frame. What a hefty
value proposition for a paper. Absolutely

00:01:58.429 --> 00:02:00.270
spectacular.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:06.960
Some of the results really remind me of topological
calculations. Topology is a subfield of mathematics

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.370
that studies what properties of different
shapes are preserved when these shapes are

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:16.780
undergoing deformations. It's super useful
because, for instance, if we can prove that

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:22.830
light behaves in some way when the light source
has the shape of a disk, then if we're interested

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:29.269
in other shapes, topology can help us determine
whether all these enormous books full of theorems

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:34.680
on other shapes are going to apply to this
shape or not. It may be that we don't need

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.760
to invent anything and can just use this vast
existing knowledge base.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.230
Some of the authors of this paper work at
Unity, which means that we can expect these

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:47.700
awesome results to appear in the video games
of the future.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.349
Some code and demos are also available on
their website which I've linked in the description

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:54.200
box, make sure to check them out!

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.920
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

